    categories: ["conflitti", "geopolitica"],
    title: "Iran foreign minister wants diplomacy to end war; Trump says terms unacceptable",
    summary: "Iranian Foreign Minister Abbas Araqchi said Tehran wanted guarantees that Washington was serious about negotiations before proceeding further.",
    preview: "Iranian Foreign Minister Abbas Araqchi said Tehran still hoped diplomacy could end its war with the United States, while U.S. President Donald Trump said Iran wanted a deal but one that was not acceptable to him. Araqchi told the state-owned Iran newspaper that messages continued to be exchanged between Iran and Washington through mediators, and that Tehran was seeking “an agreement that would ensure there was no further war.” He also said Iran wanted guarantees that Washington was serious about negotiations before proceeding further, adding that Iran…",
    body: "Iranian Foreign Minister Abbas Araqchi said Tehran still hoped diplomacy could end its war with the United States, while U.S. President Donald Trump said Iran wanted a deal but one that was not acceptable to him.\n\nAraqchi told the state-owned Iran newspaper that messages continued to be exchanged between Iran and Washington through mediators, and that Tehran was seeking “an agreement that would ensure there was no further war.”\n\nHe also said Iran wanted guarantees that Washington was serious about negotiations before proceeding further, adding that Iran had discussed with mediators the form and nature of confidence-building measures.\n\nThe remarks came as diplomatic efforts continued to try to end a conflict that has destabilized the Middle East, disrupted shipping, driven up global energy prices and drawn in countries across the region.\n\nThe foreign minister said Iran’s red lines included keeping its highly enriched uranium in the country, though he said a “fair solution” was still possible.\n\nAuthorities have previously mentioned “feasible formulas” to resolve the dispute between Tehran and Washington over the matter, including diluting the highly enriched uranium stockpile under the supervision of the International Atomic Energy Agency.\n\nThe IAEA estimates that Iran had 440.9 kilograms of uranium enriched to 60% when Israel and the U.S. attacked Iranian nuclear facilities in June 2025. How much of that has survived is unclear.\n\n“I believe a fair solution can be reached on the issue of nuclear material, one that is based on our national interests while also meeting the country’s needs,” Araqchi said.\n\nTrump, in a bews conference on Friday, said: “They want to make a deal, but they don’t want to make a deal that’s acceptable to me.”\n\nThe United States and Israel attacked Iran on Feb. 28, prompting Tehran to strike Israel and Gulf states hosting U.S. military bases.\n\nQatari and Iranian officials met on Tuesday in talks aimed at “bridging perspectives and narrowing the gap between proposals presented on the ground,” a spokesperson for Qatar’s Foreign Ministry said.",
