    body: "After outages hit US banks and aviation, FBI database reported hacked Submitted by MEE staff on Sat, 11/21/2026 - 14:57 The database holds information on warrants and identities of fugitives, with experts warning that hackers could alter records relied on by police FBI director Kash Patel is sworn in before testifying at a Senate Judiciary Committee hearing on Capitol Hill in Washington, DC, 15 September 2026 (Roberto Schmidt/AFP) Off The FBI is investigating a breach of its nationwide criminal information database, reported after a series of major technology disruptions in recent weeks.\n\nThe intrusion into the National Crime Information Center (NCIC), which federal, state and local law enforcement agencies use to share criminal justice information, was discovered in recent days, CNN reported . The FBI has called it a central law enforcement information system used by agencies across the country. The potential consequences extend beyond the theft of sensitive information. A former FBI official who led investigations into cybercriminal groups said that officers rely on the database to identify fugitives, check outstanding warrants and access information.\n\n“My concern isn’t simply whether hackers gained access to sensitive information, although that is an issue, but whether they could compromise the accuracy of the information that officers rely on,” the former official told CNN. FBI director Kash Patel addressed the bureau's cybersecurity efforts on Fox News on Thursday, after officials had learned of the latest intrusion. Patel touted Operation Winter Shield , an initiative launched earlier this year to encourage companies to strengthen their digital defences and protect sensitive information.\n\n“Other administrations could have made these updates, but they didn’t,” he said. “We are staying ahead of the curve and making up for lost time.” The FBI launched Operation Winter Shield in February as a cybersecurity campaign aimed at helping private-sector organisations strengthen their information technology and operational technology systems. A week of outages The FBI incident has unfolded alongside a series of technology disruptions affecting major US institutions.\n\nOn 13 November, the Federal Aviation Administration (FAA) ordered a nationwide ground stop after a technical problem disrupted its air traffic communications network, grounding flights for several hours . US air travel thrown into chaos as nationwide flights grounded over 'radar instability' ng to Reuters. The FAA said the problem began when its legacy telecommunications system became unstable after receiving an unusually high volume of data through its network.\n\nDays earlier, a global outage affecting Microsoft's services disrupted Outlook, Teams and its Copilot assistant, affecting organisations across several countries. The outage affected businesses, banks, hospitals, courts, airlines and government agencies. Downdetector received reports from users in more than 70 countries and territories. On 16 November, customers reported difficulties accessing online services at several major US banks, including JPMorgan Chase, Bank of America, Wells Fargo and US Bank.\n\nThe following day, the National Security Agency's classified internal communications network, known as NSANet, also experienced an outage . US Politics News Post Date Override 0 Update Date Mon, 05/04/2020 - 21:19 Update Date Override 0",
