    categories: ["ai"],
    title: "U.S. Agriculture Department and FBI breached by hackers, reports say",
    summary: "The reports come as concerns deepened over the recent spate of tech outages at key American institutions.",
    preview: "Washington – The U.S. Agriculture Department and the FBI were breached by hackers in cyberattacks, U.S. media reported Friday, as concerns deepened over the recent spate of tech outages at key American institutions. The incidents were first reported by NBC News and CNN respectively, and it remained unclear whether they were related and who was responsible. Major outages in recent weeks have disrupted travel, communications and e-commerce for countless users across the United States, even affecting a top-secret network at the National Security Agency.",
    body: "Washington – The U.S. Agriculture Department and the FBI were breached by hackers in cyberattacks, U.S. media reported Friday, as concerns deepened over the recent spate of tech outages at key American institutions.\n\nThe incidents were first reported by NBC News and CNN respectively, and it remained unclear whether they were related and who was responsible.\n\nMajor outages in recent weeks have disrupted travel, communications and e-commerce for countless users across the United States, even affecting a top-secret network at the National Security Agency.\n\nU.S. President Donald Trump has sought to calm the public and urged people to “relax,” while officials said the various outages were not linked to criminal activity.\n\nThe U.S. Department of Agriculture (USDA) told employees that a system used to facilitate payments to farmers was targeted in a cyberattack, NBC reported on Friday, citing two former USDA officials.\n\nThe hackers may use the payments system as a backdoor to access more sensitive material, the former officials told NBC.\n\nThe USDA deals with “a bunch of scary biologic issues” and “if an adversary or a competitor were to have access to that same information ... they could use our own research against us,” a former official was quoted as saying.\n\nCNN said separately on Friday that the FBI was hacked for the second time since September.\n\nThe federal agency was investigating an intrusion of the National Crime Information Center (NCIC) by apparent hackers, CNN reported citing two people briefed on the probe.\n\nThe NCIC has a nationwide criminal information database linking federal, state and local law enforcement and is used to share information on warrants and background checks.\n\n“Every day, officers across the country rely on NCIC to identify wanted fugitives, check for outstanding warrants, and access information that can directly impact officer and public safety,” a former FBI official told CNN.\n\nThe former official expressed concerns that hackers “could compromise the accuracy of the information that officers rely on.”\n\nThe newly uncovered breaches at the FBI and USDA were described as hacks, which are different from outages like the ones that afflicted Microsoft Outlook and the Federal Aviation Administration.\n\nIn September, a hacking group known as ShinyHunters targeted sensitive employee personal data from an FBI jobs website.\n\nThe White House fired cybersecurity chief Sean Cairncross this week as criticism mounted over the string of major outages.",
    content_status: "full",
    source: "The Japan Times",
    url: "https://www.japantimes.co.jp/news/2026/11/21/world/crime-legal/us-fbi-usda-cyberattacks",
